cards.en:
When conditions are nested only in the "else" branch, Python offers <b>if…elif…else</b> (pseudocode: <code>if … then … else if … then … else</code>). This multi-way selection <b>checks the conditions in the given order until the first condition that is True</b>; its statement is executed and the conditional statement ends. <b>If none is true, the "else" body runs.</b>
<pre>mark = 70
if mark >= 85:
    grade = "A"
elif mark >= 50:
    grade = "B"
else:
    grade = "F"
print(grade)     # B</pre>
Key points: <b>only one branch runs</b>; order matters (put the highest threshold first when using &gt;=). Because a later condition is reached only when earlier ones are False, <b>only the lower limit needs checking</b> (e.g. <code>elif mark &gt;= 50</code> already implies mark &lt; 85).

